From f4990c1f8e3cfd1a88e6c3d3970368c352540a45 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin Vassantachart <jwv@stanford.edu>
Date: Fri, 22 May 2026 11:19:09 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] feat: format raw C/C++ arrays in the variables view
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Adds an `ArrayFormatter` that matches `TypeDeclaration::Array` and
exposes the array elements as indexed children (`[0]`, `[1]`, …) so
locals like `int arr[5]` no longer render as `<unknown>` with no
expansion. Without this formatter, no entry in the formatter registry
matched `TypeDeclaration::Array`, so `Type::formatter()` returned
`None` and `Variable::display()` fell through to `<unknown>`.

Also fixes two DWARF parsing bugs that prevented the array length from
ever reaching the formatter or the type display:

1. `array_bound` only accepted `Udata`/`Sdata` gimli forms, but clang
   emits `DW_AT_count` / `DW_AT_upper_bound` as `DW_FORM_data1..8`,
   so those bounds parsed as `None` and arrays rendered as `T[]`.
2. `parse_subrange` stored a `DW_AT_count` value directly in
   `upper_bound`, off by one from the inclusive-index semantics
   used by `decl_name`. Now convert count → upper = lower + count - 1
   before returning, so the rest of the engine can treat the result
   uniformly regardless of which DWARF form the producer chose.

Adds a regression test that exercises an `int[5]` local: verifies the
type renders as `int[5]`, `indexedVariables = 5`, and that indexed
slices (`filter: "indexed"`, paged) return the correct elements.

Verified end-to-end in the Nova IDE — `arr` now shows as `int[5]`
@0xfffe0 and expands to `[0]=10 .. [4]=50`.
